{
  "rules": [
    "When sentiment is near zero (0.00) but recent history shows a sharp upward spike followed by volatility, the model's linear upward forecast significantly overshoots; apply a downward correction of 5-10% to account for mean reversion.",
    "The model exhibits strong momentum bias during low-sentiment periods, failing to capture sudden reversals (e.g., Example 3 and 4 where prices dropped from ~89 to ~77 despite forecasts continuing to rise); reduce forecasted growth rate by 20-30% when sentiment is flat.",
    "In high-volatility regimes with mixed or low sentiment, the model underestimates downside risk (overshoots highs) and overestimates upside potential; apply a volatility adjustment that reduces the slope of the forecast curve.",
    "When sentiment history shows isolated positive spikes (0.20-0.33) amidst otherwise neutral sentiment, the model ignores these as catalysts for immediate price jumps (as seen in Example 5); incorporate sentiment magnitude as an additive shock to the first few days of the forecast horizon."
  ]
}
